# CollabHub Auth — manual API walkthrough
#
# For the VS Code REST Client extension (humao.rest-client). "Send Request" appears
# above each request; run them top to bottom, because most of them feed the next one
# by referencing an earlier response body — no copying tokens by hand.
#
# Assumes the stack is up:
#
#     docker compose up -d postgres redis-cache dex auth
#
# Auth is on 8001 (AUTH_PORT in .env). It needs Postgres, Redis Cache (R1) and Dex —
# no Messaging, no Garage, no Elasticsearch.
#
# SIGNING IN IS NOT IN THIS FILE, and cannot be. Since register D5 was settled, the
# only way in is the OIDC authorization-code flow: three redirects and an HTML login
# form at Dex, which a .http file cannot drive. Get a session first:
#
#     ./scripts/sign-in.py                     # ada@collabhub.dev
#     ./scripts/sign-in.py grace@collabhub.dev # a second user
#
# then paste the values into the variables below. Signing in through the SPA at
# http://localhost:5173 works just as well.
#
# THE REFRESH TOKEN IS A COOKIE, not a body field (register D22). A browser holds
# it as HttpOnly and never reveals it; `sign-in.py --refresh-cookie` prints the
# value precisely so this file can send it as a `Cookie` header by hand. That is a
# development affordance and not something any client should do.
#
# Two things worth knowing before you start:
#
# * Refresh tokens rotate. Each one works exactly once, so re-running a request out
#   of order will 401 — that is the design, not a broken file. Run sign-in.py again
#   for a fresh session, and note that every renewal below returns a *new* cookie
#   in its Set-Cookie header that the next request would need.
# * Access tokens live 15 minutes and are signed with a key generated at startup
#   unless AUTH_SIGNING_KEY is set. Restart the container and every token before it
#   becomes invalid.

@host = http://localhost:8001
@json = application/json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Health
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Liveness — is the process up. Checks nothing.
GET {{host}}/health/live

### Readiness — checks Postgres and Redis Cache. 503 until both answer.
GET {{host}}/health/ready

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWKS — how the rest of the platform trusts these tokens
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The public keys. Every other service fetches this and verifies signatures
# locally, so no request on the platform is ever routed through Auth.
# `kid` is an RFC 7638 thumbprint, so it is the same in every replica.
# Cacheable on purpose — check the Cache-Control header on the response.
GET {{host}}/.well-known/jwks.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing in — paste from ./scripts/sign-in.py (see the notes at the top)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

@accessToken = PASTE_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE
@refreshCookie = PASTE_REFRESH_COOKIE_HERE

# A second user, for the requests that need someone to add or switch alongside.
# From: ./scripts/sign-in.py grace@collabhub.dev
@graceAccessToken = PASTE_GRACE_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE
@graceRefreshCookie = PASTE_GRACE_REFRESH_COOKIE_HERE

### Where a sign-in begins. Sending this returns a 302 to Dex rather than JSON —
# useful for seeing the state, nonce and PKCE challenge Auth generates, but the
# browser has to take it from here.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login/dex?codeChallenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&codeChallengeMethod=S256

### PKCE is mandatory — no challenge is a 400.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login/dex

### `plain` PKCE is refused: the challenge would be the verifier in clear.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login/dex?codeChallenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM&codeChallengeMethod=plain

### An unknown provider. Expect 404.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/login/okta?codeChallenge=E9Melhoa2OwvFrEMTJguCHaoeK1t8URWbuGJSstw-cM

### A callback with a state that was never issued. Expect a 302 back to the SPA
# carrying `error=invalid_state` — browser endpoints redirect, they do not raise.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/callback/dex?code=anything&state=never-issued

### An authorization code that was never issued. Expect 401.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "authorization_code",
  "code": "never-issued",
  "codeVerifier": "dBjftJeZ4CVP-mB92K27uhbUJU1p1r_wW1gFWFOEjXk"
}

### The signed-in user's profile.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### The claims on the current token — no database read.
# `wsp` is the one workspace this token is scoped to; `roles` is the role in
# that workspace only.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/auth/userinfo
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Workspaces Ada belongs to, with her role in each. This is what the workspace
# switcher reads. Signing in lands her in the one she owns.
# @name workspaces
GET {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Refresh and rotation
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Trade the refresh token for a new pair. The old refresh token is spent by
# this call; the new access token stays in the same workspace.
# @name refreshed
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{refreshCookie}}

### Replay the token that was just spent. Expect 401 — and this does more than
# refuse: reuse means a copy is in circulation, so the whole rotation family is
# revoked. Run the request below afterwards to see the *newest* token dead too.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{refreshCookie}}

### The token issued by the successful refresh above. On its own this would work;
# after the replay it is 401, because the family went down with it.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Cookie: collabhub_rt=PASTE_THE_ROTATED_COOKIE_FROM_THE_SET_COOKIE_ABOVE

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Switching workspace
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Ada's session is burnt after the replay above, so run ./scripts/sign-in.py
# again and paste the new pair here.
@freshRefreshCookie = PASTE_A_FRESH_REFRESH_COOKIE_HERE

### Re-scope the session to another workspace. Same rotation as /refresh — the
# only difference is the `wsp` and `roles` claims on the new token.
#
# `items[0]` is whichever workspace is listed first. To watch the role change
# from "owner" to "member", paste the id of "CollabHub Demo" from the
# /api/v1/workspaces response above, then decode the token at jwt.io.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/switch-workspace
Content-Type: {{json}}
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{freshRefreshCookie}}

{
  "workspaceId": "{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}"
}

### Try to switch into a workspace you are not a member of. Expect 403.
# The target comes from the request, so it is only ever trusted after a
# membership lookup against the refresh token's own user — holding someone
# else's workspace id gets you nothing.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/switch-workspace
Content-Type: {{json}}
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{graceRefreshCookie}}

{
  "workspaceId": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Profiles
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Update your own profile. There is no PATCH /users/{id} — the row edited comes
# from the token's `sub`, so there is no id in the request to substitute.
PATCH {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "displayName": "Ada Lovelace"
}

### Clear the avatar. An omitted field means "leave alone"; an explicit null means
# "clear" — without the distinction an avatar could be set but never removed.
PATCH {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "avatarAsset": null
}

### Someone else's public profile — name and avatar, never their email. Only
# visible if you share a workspace with them, or a workspace-scoped token would
# be a directory of every tenant. Paste a user id from the members list below.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workspace membership
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### The members of the workspace this token is scoped to. Cursor-paginated
# (Conventions §4.1) — `?limit=` defaults to 50, maximum 200.
# @name members
GET {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members?limit=2
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### The next page. Paste `nextCursor` from the response above; a null one means
# there is nothing more.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members?limit=2&cursor=PASTE_NEXT_CURSOR
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### A workspace the token does not name. Expect 403 — the path workspace must
# equal the `wsp` claim (Conventions §5.4), not merely be one you belong to.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000/members
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

### Add an existing user. This is not an invitation: there is no invitations
# table, so an address nobody has signed in with is a 404, not a pending invite.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "grace@collabhub.dev",
  "role": "member"
}

### Adding them twice. Expect 409.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "grace@collabhub.dev",
  "role": "member"
}

### Promote them. Paste their user id from the members list above.
PATCH {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members/PASTE_USER_ID
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "role": "admin"
}

### Demote the workspace's only owner. Expect 409 — a workspace with no owner has
# nobody left who can grant the role back.
PATCH {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members/PASTE_YOUR_OWN_USER_ID
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "role": "member"
}

### Remove them. Expect 204 — and their refresh tokens for *this* workspace are
# revoked, so the session it granted them cannot be renewed. Their access token
# stays valid for the rest of its 15 minutes (Conventions §5.2).
DELETE {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members/PASTE_USER_ID
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Signing out
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### A session to sign out of — another ./scripts/sign-in.py pair.
@endingAccessToken = PASTE_ANOTHER_ACCESS_TOKEN_HERE
@endingRefreshCookie = PASTE_ANOTHER_REFRESH_COOKIE_HERE

### Still valid — expect 200.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{endingAccessToken}}

### Sign out. Expect 204. The access token stays cryptographically valid until it
# expires — that is what stateless verification means — so this writes its `jti`
# to the denylist in R1 with a TTL of whatever life it had left. The refresh
# token the cookie carries is revoked too, and the response clears the cookie.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/logout
Authorization: Bearer {{endingAccessToken}}
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{endingRefreshCookie}}

### The same token, now on the denylist. Expect 401.
# `docker compose exec redis-cache redis-cli --scan --pattern 'auth:revoked:*'`
# shows the entry.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{endingAccessToken}}

### The refresh token that was handed to logout. Expect 401.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/refresh
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{endingRefreshCookie}}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Service tokens — how background work calls other services
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### Client-credentials grant. The secret comes from AUTH_SERVICE_CLIENTS in .env.
# The token that comes back carries `aud: collabhub-internal`, `sub:
# service:worker` and `scp` scopes, with no workspace at all — decode it and
# compare with a user token.
# @name serviceToken
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "worker-local-secret"
}

### A service token on a user endpoint. Expect 401 — the audience check that runs
# on every request is the whole boundary between background work and a user's
# session. It works in the other direction too: a user token can never satisfy
# an /api/v1/internal/ endpoint.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
Authorization: Bearer {{serviceToken.response.body.$.accessToken}}

### Wrong secret. Expect 401.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "not-the-secret"
}

### Unknown client. Expect 401 — and the same message as the wrong secret above,
# because which of the two failed is useful only to someone guessing.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "client_credentials",
  "clientId": "ghost",
  "clientSecret": "anything"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The error envelope
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

### No token. Expect 401 as application/problem+json (RFC 7807), with a `traceId`
# that ties the response to the log line and the trace.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me

### A field that fails validation. Expect 400 with an `errors` map keyed by field.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/workspaces/{{workspaces.response.body.$.items[0].id}}/members
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "email": "not-an-email-address",
  "role": "member"
}

### snake_case where camelCase is expected. Expect 400: JSON on this platform is
# camelCase everywhere, and accepting both spellings would leave two wire
# formats in circulation that no document describes.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/switch-workspace
Content-Type: {{json}}
Cookie: collabhub_rt={{refreshCookie}}

{
  "workspace_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000"
}

### An unsupported grant type. Expect 400.
POST {{host}}/api/v1/auth/service-token
Content-Type: {{json}}

{
  "grantType": "password",
  "clientId": "worker",
  "clientSecret": "worker-local-secret"
}

### Send a trace id and get it back on the problem document — this is how an error
# a user reports leads to the trace in Tempo and the logs in Loki.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/users/me
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01
